WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.266
FedRAMP is built on NIST guidance, particularly NIST Special Publication 800-53.

2
00:00:06.266 --> 00:00:15.455
NIST SP 800-53 provides a catalog of security and privacy controls used across federal information systems and programs.

3
00:00:15.455 --> 00:00:22.061
In FedRAMP, those controls provide the foundation for many security requirements and assessment activities.

4
00:00:22.061 --> 00:00:28.057
They help define what a cloud service provider needs to implement, document, assess, and monitor.

5
00:00:28.057 --> 00:00:43.383
Controls cover many areas, including access control, incident response, configuration management, contingency planning, identification and authentication, audit logging, risk assessment, system communications, and vulnerability management.

6
00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:49.948
The NIST relationship is important because FedRAMP is not invented separately from federal risk management practices.

7
00:00:49.948 --> 00:00:54.957
It is connected to the same control language used in broader federal cybersecurity governance.

8
00:00:54.957 --> 00:01:07.020
Cloud providers and agencies use these controls to structure security plans, implementation statements, assessment procedures, findings, remediation plans, and monitoring activities.

9
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.482
Control implementation is not just a paperwork activity.

10
00:01:10.482 --> 00:01:19.372
A control may involve technical configuration, operational procedures, policy, monitoring, evidence generation, and clear ownership.

11
00:01:19.372 --> 00:01:29.879
For learners, the key takeaway is that FedRAMP translates NIST control expectations into a standardized cloud security assessment and monitoring model for federal use.

12
00:01:29.879 --> 00:01:32.573
This connection also helps technical teams.

13
00:01:32.573 --> 00:01:45.324
When engineers understand the control families behind FedRAMP requirements, they can connect architecture, configuration, logging, identity, and operational practices to specific risk objectives.

14
00:01:45.324 --> 00:01:49.525
The control language provides a bridge between implementation and governance.

15
00:01:49.525 --> 00:01:58.186
It lets a technical safeguard become part of a larger story about how the cloud service protects federal information and supports agency risk decisions.
